WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.782
Date columns often look like dates when we read a CSV file, but Pandas may still store them as text.

00:00:07.782 --> 00:00:15.137
Before doing reliable time-based analysis, convert the column with pandas to datetime.

00:00:15.137 --> 00:00:22.907
Once the values are real datetime objects, the dt accessor gives us many useful features.

00:00:22.907 --> 00:00:28.973
In the example, we create the weekday name and a monthly period from the date column.

00:00:29.602 --> 00:00:34.699
These features are useful because many analytical questions are based on time.

00:00:34.699 --> 00:00:41.684
We may want to compare weekdays and weekends, calculate monthly totals, study seasonal patterns,

00:00:41.684 --> 00:00:45.044
or filter records within a specific date range.

00:00:45.044 --> 00:00:48.544
Always inspect the result after converting dates.

00:00:48.544 --> 00:00:55.619
Real-world date columns can contain missing values, inconsistent formats, or invalid dates.

00:00:55.619 --> 00:01:01.084
Run the example and then try adding a year column or a numerical month column.

00:01:01.084 --> 00:01:06.395
The dt accessor gives you many options once the date has been converted correctly.
